**Diagnosis: the build failed at the right place and nothing was deployed.** No image was created, so the MIG and running servers are untouched.

**Evidence from the log**
- All 18 secrets fail with `PERMISSION_DENIED ... secretmanager.versions.access`. Nothing else in the build is broken: apt, Node, Apache, Ops Agent and PM2 all succeeded.
- The app crashes at `loadSecrets`, so `/health` never answers and the build fails after 6 retries.
- The `.env.staging ENOENT` line is only the fallback warning, not the cause.
- The `sa_1086...` user in the log is the identity Packer used for OS Login. It is not the VM's service account, so the log doesn't show which account the VM ran as.

I won't name the root cause until two things are checked. Either the VM ran as `sa-cn-backend` and that account lacks Secret Accessor, or the Packer edit didn't apply and the VM ran as a different account.

**Run these (info only) and paste the output**
```bash
P=pg-ae-n-app-125609

# 1. In the backend repo: confirm the edit is in the pushed commit
grep -n "service_account_email" packer/*.pkr.hcl
git log -1 --stat

# 2. Which regional CN_STAGING_* secrets have sa-cn-backend (count 1 = yes, 0 = no)
for s in $(gcloud secrets list --location=me-central2 --project $P --filter="name~CN_STAGING_" --format="value(name.basename())"); do
  echo -n "$s: "
  gcloud secrets get-iam-policy $s --location=me-central2 --project $P --format="value(bindings.members)" | grep -c sa-cn-backend
done

# 3. Full policy on one secret, to see which roles and members are bound
gcloud secrets get-iam-policy CN_STAGING_DB_HOST --location=me-central2 --project $P
```

**How to read the results**
| Result | Meaning | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Step 1 shows `sa-cn-backend`, and step 2 shows 0 for most or all secrets | Secret Accessor was never granted | Grant it (see below) |
| Step 1 shows `sa-cn-backend`, and step 2 shows 1 for all secrets | The binding exists, so the cause is elsewhere (loader endpoint, or secret names don't match) | Paste the `secret-manager.ts` client setup |
| Step 1 shows nothing or the wrong account | The edit wasn't pushed | Re-apply it and rebuild |

**Grant, if step 2 shows 0** (needs an IAM admin, since your account can't edit IAM)
Console → Security → Secret Manager → switch the location filter to `me-central2` → tick all `CN_STAGING_*` secrets → Permissions panel → Add principal → `sa-cn-backend@pg-ae-n-app-125609.iam.gserviceaccount.com` → role **Secret Manager Secret Accessor** → Save.

- **Per-secret binding (recommended):** least privilege. It costs an extra step whenever a new secret is added.
- **Project-level binding:** one grant, but the account can read every secret in the project, which violates least privilege. Avoid it.

**Rollback:** none needed. After the grant, re-run the same build. It is safe to retry.

Also needed on your side: secret names in the log are the app's keys (`DB_HOST`), while the secrets are `CN_STAGING_*`. If step 2 shows 1 for all secrets, I'll need that loader snippet to check the mapping.
